# Design & Industrial Engineering Curricular Hub
#
# Root cache index for undergraduate and master's degree programs across industrial design, operations research, human factors, and manufacturing systems.

#>spw_cache_univ_design_ie_index
#:cache #!design_and_industrial_engineering
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"intent"{
  ~#goal: "Model the full pedagogical continuum from human-centered industrial design, physical ergonomics, and form-giving to mathematical operations research, stochastic queueing, and global supply chain systems."
  ~#accreditation: "IDSA (Industrial Designers Society of America), ABET Engineering Accreditation Commission Criteria for Industrial Engineering"
  ~#protocol: "cache.layer/1"
}

^"roots"{
  @undergrad_industrial_design: ~"./undergrad-industrial-design.spw"
  @undergrad_industrial_engineering: ~"./undergrad-industrial-engineering.spw"
  @masters_design_systems: ~"./masters-design-and-industrial-systems.spw"
  @taste: ~"./taste.spw"
}

^"dispatch"{
  undergrad_industrial_design: @undergrad_industrial_design
  undergrad_industrial_engineering: @undergrad_industrial_engineering
  masters_design_and_industrial_systems: @masters_design_systems
  taste_considerations: @taste
}

^"taste"{
  ~#taste: "Design gives products a human heart; industrial engineering builds the circulatory system that brings them to billions of people without waste."
  ~#wonder: "Why does shaving ten seconds off a worker's repetitive assembly movement prevent chronic musculoskeletal injury while saving a factory millions of dollars a year?"
}

^"invariants"{
  "Industrial design curricula balance aesthetic form-giving with empirical anthropometric testing and manufacturing viability.",
  "Industrial engineering operations research models must demonstrate formal mathematical optimality and proof of convergence.",
  "System optimization must prioritize the physical safety, cognitive load, and human dignity of workers over raw throughput.",
}

#>wonder_design_ie_index_1
?["How does the early pedagogical integration of ergonomic physical mockups into industrial design studios reduce upper-limb repetitive strain injuries in consumer tools?"]{
  #:depth #!ergonomic ~#lens(haptic_anthropometrics)
  ~#hypothesis: "Industrial design students who sculpt and user-test high-density foam handles with electromyographic (EMG) forearm feedback design garden and culinary tools that reduce carpal tunnel wrist deviation by 35% compared to students designing purely in 3D CAD on screens."
  !probe{ "Measure surface EMG muscle activation and wrist flexion/extension angles in users testing hand tools designed via tactile foam vs CAD-only design workflows." }
  $%[ergonomics.carpal_angle_deviation_degrees, biomechanics.forearm_emg_peak_microvolts]
}
